AB  - The chapter addresses the topic of SDG 11 with the goal of understanding the tools and solutions that can render cities and communities more sustainable within the framework of five key concepts of Agenda 2023 (People, Planet, Prosperity, Peace, and Partnership). The evolution towards sustainable cities is a crucial stride for creating a green and competitive urban environment, focusing on livability, access to public services, and social well-being. The chapter outlines the concept of a sustainable smart city, as an innovative urban setting designed to improve citizens' quality of life through interconnected and environmentally friendly digital solutions. Green logistics is an integral component of the development of this type of cities. In designing sustainable smart cities, the SDG public–private partnership plays a strategic role in initiating a forum for dialogue and achieving shared planning of urban areas. Considering the growing diffusion of partnerships and the lack of systems to evaluate their actions, in 2023 the Economic and Social Council presented a methodology for assessing the sustainable development objectives of public–private partnerships, defining an Infrastructure Evaluation and Rating System. The chapter pays particular attention to this emerging theme and highlights the necessity to adopt theoretical models and managerial practices to achieve a holistic view of sustainability. In this regard, the chapter proposes the Nature-Based Solution and the Natural Step, as promising and alternative approaches that can guide managers and public actors. In both models, decisions revolve around nature as a resource producing social benefits. In conclusion, the chapter provides food for thought to formulate strategies and embrace public–private solutions for building cities of the future responsibly.
