AB  - Esports, or competitive video gaming, is a product of digital globalization: a borderless industry built on games owned by transnational publishers and watched by a global audience, yet governed unevenly by national legal systems. We examine this global-local tension by comparing how three Asian countries at different points on the development spectrum regulate the same globally distributed activity. Combining a doctrinal analysis of statutes, gazette notifications, and federation rules with ordinal coding of fourteen regulatory dimensions, we also analyze 291 full-text news articles (2018–2025) using Latent Dirichlet Allocation topic modeling and lexicon-based sentiment analysis to capture how reform is received in the media. South Korea, an advanced economy and early mover, reached an esports-specific regulatory formalization index of 96.4% of the attainable maximum; India, a large emerging economy that has taken a restrictive turn, 64.3%; and Bangladesh, a frontier market that recognized esports only in 2025, 35.7%, weakest in player rights and institutional capacity (17% of maximum in both). For Bangladesh, we derive a prioritized reform agenda targeting the dedicated statute, player contracts, athlete status, dispute resolution, and training. India and South Korea shared the same provision level on only a small share of dimensions, diverging most on dedicated statutes, player contracts, and dispute resolution. Topic modeling separated an Indian discourse dominated by the real-money gaming ban from a Korean discourse centered on an established competitive ecosystem, and Korean coverage was significantly more positive (93.6% versus 80.2% positive; Mann-Whitney U = 6780, p < 0.001). The ordering proved robust to how the index is constructed. Across these three cases, regulatory formalization and media sentiment moved together, and the emerging economies faced a predictable sequence of legal gaps. We offer a transferable, low-cost instrument for benchmarking regulatory formalization and a concrete reform agenda for developing countries seeking to capture the developmental benefits of a globalized digital sector.
