AB  - Autoencoder (AE)-based models represent a diverse family of architectures designed for unsupervised learning, structured representation compression, controllable data synthesis, etc. Although they are widely applied across image, text, and speech modalities, their latent behavior and generative dynamics remain under-characterized in existing reviews. This survey organizes AE variants according to the way they regulate latent structure, incorporate prior assumptions, and balance reconstruction accuracy with generative diversity. We examine deterministic, probabilistic, adversarial, and diffusion-based AEs through representative architectures, including Sparse, Denoising, and Convolutional Autoencoders; Variational Autoencoder (VAE) derivatives, e.g., β-VAE, Conditional VAE, Vector Quantized VAE, Wasserstein AE; adversarial frameworks, e.g., Adversarial AE, Introspective VAE, and Adversarial Generator–Encoder Network, VAE-Generative Adversarial Network (VAE-GAN), Bidirectional GAN; and diffusion-based frameworks employing latent autoencoding, e.g., Latent Diffusion Model and Diffusion Autoencoders. For each, we analyze latent constraints, architectural trade-offs, and recurring issues such as posterior collapse, mode imbalance, and codebook degradation. We also discuss hybrid architectures that combine latent inference with iterative or adversarial generation strategies. Further, case studies in image inpainting, speech denoising, and topic modeling illustrate how latent mechanisms affect learning behavior and application outcomes. Overall, this review consolidates recent advances through a unified latent-space-centered taxonomy and comparative analysis, providing a comprehensive perspective on AE-based generative frameworks across diverse application domains.
ER  - 

