AB  - This study examines how employer demand for AI talent in China changed from 2018 to 2025 and how the diffusion of generative AI reshaped recruitment requirements, skill composition, and city distribution in this context. Online job postings are treated as empirical evidence of employers' stated recruitment intentions. This study, which draws on more than one million postings obtained from major recruitment platforms, combines text mining, trend analysis, topic modelling, and a dynamic difference-in-differences analysis. The results indicate a structural adjustment in AI talent demand, which is reflected in changes in recruitment requirements, skill composition, and spatial distribution. AI talent recruitment demand became more specialized, engineering-oriented, and selective, including an increasing emphasis on AI technical capabilities, communication and coordination skills, and higher education and experience thresholds. Bachelor's degrees remained the dominant baseline requirement, whereas postings increasingly indicated demand for postgraduate qualifications and candidates with one to five years of experience. Topic modelling suggests that foundational algorithmic and engineering capabilities remained central, whereas large language models, generative AI, deep learning frameworks, and robotics became prominent after 2022. The post-2022 pattern suggests a staged and asymmetric adjustment, which is characterized by broader diffusion from first-tier cities to new first-tier and lower-tier cities and stronger adjustment in cities that featured higher levels of prior AI exposure. These findings provide demand-side evidence concerning how technological change is reflected in recruitment requirements and skill composition and have implications for higher education alignment, workforce development, and adaptive talent policy.
