AB  - Advancing from just being a microsystem technology spin-off, microfluidics established itself as a multifaceted technology over recent year. Breaking from its initial roles for on-chip capillary-based electrophoresis, microfluidics has facilitated discovery of new phenomena taking advantage of large surface-to-volume ratio, large optical/electrical/acoustic field gradients, and facile thermal diffusion. Microfluidic devices allow fluid transportation and manipulation of extremely low volumes, per say, to understand an underlying chemical or biological reaction. Realization of a microfluidic device is inclusive of physical, chemical, and biological phenomena in addition to fabrication methodologies. Through this chapter, a broad overview of different parameters that influence microfluidics as a science or technology is presented. The prelude of the chapter focuses on physical phenomenon defining fluidic behavior at microscale. Advances in microfabrication routes, applications, with emphasis on diagnostics and current examples of academic and commercial microfluidic devices are detailed sequentially thereafter. A conclusive summary sets forth a dialog to address the challenges such as device integration, hardware, or commercial acceptance towards developing a practical diagnostic tool.
