AB  - Crack segmentation aims to accurately depict small, low-contrast crack patterns against complex backgrounds. However, crack pixels typically exhibit weak gradients and discontinuities, making stable representation difficult. Existing methods typically rely on multi-scale fusion or attention mechanisms to alleviate this problem, but they still struggle to preserve fine-grained boundary details, leading to missed detections and fragmented predictions. Therefore, this paper proposes a dual-stream multi-scale information retention framework centered on detail retention (DRCNet). Unlike methods that operate only in the spatial domain and risk oversmoothing, DRCNet introduces a structure-enhanced Perona-Malik (SEPM) branch at the encoder end, which uses anisotropic diffusion to explicitly enhance edge and orientation details; meanwhile, a multi-scale information extraction (MSE) branch is also introduced to aggregate complementary context through parallel receptive fields. In the cross-stream rotational encoding (CRE) module, two feature streams are aligned and interact effectively with each other. During the decoding process, high-resolution low-level features from the backbone network are injected to enhance spatial awareness, and cross-entropy loss and Dice loss are combined to alleviate class imbalance, while emphasizing the learning of slender structures. Extensive experiments on benchmarks including Crack500, DeepCrack, CrackForest, and CTCD demonstrate that DRCNet consistently outperforms robust baseline models in metrics such as mIoU and F1-score, and remains robust in challenging scenarios with cluttered backgrounds, occlusion, and resolution degradation, validating the effectiveness of the proposed method.
