AB  - The rapidly increasing volume of unstructured textual data generated on digital platforms has intensified the need for robust, interpretable, and semantically consistent topic modeling approaches. Although classical probabilistic and matrix factorization–based methods such as Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA), Latent Semantic Analysis (LSA), and Non-negative Matrix Factorization (NMF) can identify latent thematic structures, they often fail to adequately capture contextual semantics and complex linguistic relationships. However, comparative explainable evaluation frameworks integrating semantic analysis, optimization, and knowledge graph (KG)–based interpretability remain limited in the literature. To address this gap, this study proposes a comprehensive hybrid topic modeling framework that combines classical statistical models and semantic embedding–based approaches within a unified explainable evaluation pipeline. The framework integrates LDA, LSA, NMF, BERTopic, Top2Vec, and Sentence-BERT + KMeans models with grid search–based coherence optimization, semantic similarity analysis, and KG-based visualization. Experiments were conducted on the publicly available Stable Diffusion Prompts dataset containing approximately 81,000 AI-generated creative text prompts. Model performance was evaluated using c_v and u_mass coherence metrics, runtime-based efficiency analysis, semantic similarity matrices, and graph-based topic relationship analysis. Experimental results demonstrate that BERTopic achieved the highest overall coherence performance, while SBERT + KMeans produced highly balanced and semantically rich topic distributions. Among the classical statistical topic modeling approaches, NMF achieved the strongest coherence performance, whereas BERTopic demonstrated the highest overall contextual semantic interpretability and embedding-based clustering capability. Moreover, KG-based analysis revealed meaningful inter-topic semantic relationships and improved the interpretability of thematic structures through graph-based visualization. The proposed framework contributes to the literature by providing an explainable and optimization-driven comparative topic modeling architecture that bridges symbolic and semantic paradigms for large-scale text analysis.
